.TH PATHCHORD 1 "November 2026" "PathChord" "PathChord manual"
.SH NAME
pathchord \- PathChord ingress and egress proxy runtime
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pathchord
.RI [ global-options ]
.B ingress
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B pathchord
.RI [ global-options ]
.B egress
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B pathchord
.RI [ global-options ]
.B status
.br
.B pathchord keygen --output
.I identity.json
.SH DESCRIPTION
PathChord provides local HTTP forwarding, HTTP CONNECT, SOCKS4/4a/5, and
same-port mixed listeners.  The
.B ingress
command starts the local listener runtime and the
.B egress
command starts an authenticated remote Path listener.  Each service is a
separate process.
.PP
Configuration is a directory of YAML, YML, or JSON resources.  Resources are
validated as one graph and are reloaded atomically.  Private identity and
credential files must be kept outside the configuration directory.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ingress
Start the Ingress runtime.  Use
.B --config
for one resource file or
.B --config-dir
for a resource directory.
.TP
.B egress
Start the Egress runtime.  The Egress service owns its authorization database
and public Path listener.
.TP
.B status
Query the local control endpoint.
.TP
.B keygen
Create a new private static identity.  Existing output files are never
overwritten; the public key is printed to standard output.
.TP
.B enroll
Manage Egress authorization records from a protected local control endpoint.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -c, --config\  path
Use one YAML, YML, or JSON resource file.
.TP
.BI -C, --config-dir\  path
Use a configuration directory.  Subdirectories are included.
.TP
.BI --identity\  path
Use the private identity file required by an enrolled topology.
.TP
.BI --data-dir\  path
Select the runtime cache and durable data directory for Ingress.
.TP
.BI --credentials-dir\  path
Select the directory containing private credential documents.
.TP
.BI --socket\  path
Select the local Unix socket or Windows Named Pipe used for control requests.
.TP
.BI --help
Show command-specific help.
.TP
.BI --version
Show the package version.
.SH CONFIGURATION SOURCES
Command-line options take precedence over environment variables.  When no
option is supplied, PATHCHORD_CONFIG or PATHCHORD_CONFIG_DIR is used.  The
account default is
.I ~/.config/pathchord
for regular users and
.I /etc/pathchord
for root and service accounts on Linux and Alpine.  FreeBSD packages use
.I /usr/local/etc/pathchord .
On Windows the corresponding service default is under the system profile;
packaged services pass an explicit configuration directory.
.PP
Set only one of PATHCHORD_CONFIG and PATHCHORD_CONFIG_DIR.  The two variables
cannot be combined, and a directory source does not have last-file-wins
semantics.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pathchord/
System configuration directory on Linux and Alpine service installations.
.TP
.I /usr/local/etc/pathchord/
System configuration directory on FreeBSD package installations.
.TP
.I /run/pathchord/ingress.sock
Ingress local control socket on Unix.
.TP
.I /run/pathchord/egress.sock
Egress local control socket on Unix.
.TP
.I /var/run/pathchord/
FreeBSD runtime directory for the local control sockets.
.TP
.I %ProgramData%\\PathChord\\config\\
Packaged Windows service configuration directory.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
pathchord ingress --config-dir /etc/pathchord
pathchord keygen --output /var/lib/pathchord/ingress.key.json
pathchord --socket /run/pathchord/egress.sock status
.EE
.SH SECURITY
Do not place private keys, bearer tokens, or credential values in a published
configuration template.  Restrict service data and control endpoints to the
service account and administrators.  Replace the loopback default before
exposing a listener to another network.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pathchord (1),
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR rc.conf (5)
